<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fulda is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the river of the same name. It grew around the Benedictine abbey founded in 744 by Sturm, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>, who is buried there; the abbey became a great centre of Carolingian learning and later a prince-abbey and prince-bishopric. Its baroque cathedral and palace date from the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulda.jpg" alt="Fulda" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © Ra Boe / Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flug_-Nordholz-Hammelburg_2015_by-RaBoe_1053_-_Fulda_Innenstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
